From 472b758426db93206c2df130ef0f994721711b1a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joakim Hansson <joakim.hansson87@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 23:37:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 20/36] qwen4exp: incremental pooled-key cache for the QSA
 indexer

The block-sparse attention indexer scored queries against block summary
keys (mean of each 4-token block's raw keys, normalized, roped) that were
regathered and recomputed over the whole context every token, in all 12
QSA layers - the dominant remaining depth cost.

Only complete blocks are ever scored (incomplete tails ride the bias
term), and a complete block's members never change, so the summaries are
cached: one f32 row per position block per layer, written by the graph
via set_rows. Per ubatch only the freshly completed blocks (at most one
per decode step) are pooled/normed/roped; the score reads the cache.

Validity is a per-sequence block watermark clamped by seq_rm/seq_cp/
seq_add/seq_div/clear and reset by full state loads; speculative
checkpoint replay (PARTIAL_ONLY) leaves the indexer cells untouched and
its rollback arrives through seq_rm, so rejection costs nothing. Stale
rows are finite and masked by the -inf bias, exactly like the previous
garbage partial-block pools. Single-stream memories only; multi-stream
and LLAMA_QSA_NO_POOLED_CACHE=1 fall back to the full recompute (whose
blk_cells/blk_pos inputs are now only created on that path - an
unreferenced graph input is never allocated and must not be filled).

Strix Halo (RADV), plain decode: 25.7/25.2/24.2/23.5 t/s at 4k/8k/16k/
32k vs 25.1/24.0/22.4/20.0 (+17% at 32k; the depth slope mostly
flattens). MTP-speculated 5.9k summary: 31.7 -> 35.0 t/s. Greedy output
bit-identical shallow; deep divergence equals the kernel-order noise
control. Spec rollback validated over multi-turn cache-reuse requests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
